1. In your PIVIX project, policyId is a prop. When it changes, you need to fetch fresh policy details from an API. Which hook do you use, how do you write it, and how do you prevent a memory leak if the component unmounts before the response comes back?

I would use useEffect because API calls are side effects in React.

The effect depends on policyId, so whenever policyId changes, the API is called again to fetch fresh policy details.

To prevent memory leaks if the component unmounts before the API response returns, I use AbortController inside the cleanup function to cancel the pending request.


| Requirement            | Answer                                                  |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| Which hook to use?     | `useEffect`                                             |
| Why use it?            | Because API fetching is a side effect                   |
| Dependency             | `policyId`                                              |
| Trigger                | Runs whenever `policyId` changes                        |
| Memory Leak Prevention | Cleanup function with `AbortController` or mounted flag |
| Best Practice          | Cancel API request on component unmount                 |

import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

function PolicyDetails({ policyId }) {
  const [policy, setPolicy] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();

    const fetchPolicyDetails = async () => {
      try {
        const response = await fetch(
          `/api/policies/${policyId}`,
          {
            signal: controller.signal,
          }
        );

        const data = await response.json();
        setPolicy(data);
      } catch (error) {
        if (error.name !== "AbortError") {
          console.error("API Error:", error);
        }
      }
    };

    if (policyId) {
      fetchPolicyDetails();
    }

    // Cleanup function
    return () => {
      controller.abort();
    };
  }, [policyId]);

  return (
    <div>
      {policy ? policy.name : "Loading..."}
    </div>
  );
}
